course: money
lesson: 14
title: Receipts, refunds, queues and self-checkout
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A receipt is the paper or email that proves what you bought, when and for how much; Mum kept the receipt for Kian's £30 shoes, so when the sole came off a week later the shop gave her the £30 back.
explain: If something is faulty, meaning broken when it should not be, the law usually gives you a refund, a repair or a replacement; if you just change your mind, the shop does not have to take it back, though many do for unused things.
explain: Checking the receipt catches mistakes: Ava spots that they paid for 3 milks at £1.20, which is £3.60, but only got 2, worth £2.40, so the shop owes them £1.20.
explain: At a self-checkout you scan your own shopping, and it is easy to miss something by accident; if you spot it, scan it, because an unpaid loaf of bread is a loss to the shop.
fact: A receipt is proof of what you bought, when and for how much.
fact: A refund is getting your money back.
fact: Faulty means broken or not working when it should; shops must usually give a refund, repair or replacement for faulty goods.
fact: Shops do not have to refund you just because you changed your mind, though many will for unused things within a few weeks.
fact: A gift receipt lets someone swap a present without seeing its price.
fact: An itemised receipt lists each thing you bought, so you can spot mistakes.
fact: Queues get longer when fewer tills are open, because each till serves one person at a time.
fact: At a self-checkout you scan each item's barcode yourself; shops check with cameras and staff.
fact: Many UK shops charge for carrier bags so people use fewer and make less plastic rubbish.
fact: Some places take only cash, so owing a friend is fine if you pay back quickly as promised.
fact: A price match is when a shop lowers its price to match a cheaper shop; you usually have to show proof.
fact: Photos are good proof when something arrives broken from an online shop.
exercise: What is a receipt? | Proof of what you bought, when and for how much | A coupon for money off next time | A list of things you want to buy | A ticket for the queue
exercise: Kian's £30 shoes fall apart in a week. With the receipt, how much do they get back? | £30 | £15 | £0 | £60
exercise: What does "faulty" mean? | Broken or not working when it should be | Not liked by the person who bought it | Bought in a sale | Sold without a receipt
exercise: Lily's £15 birthday jumper is too small. She swaps it for a bigger £15 one. How much does she pay? | £0 | £15 | £30 | £7.50
exercise: What is a gift receipt for? | Swapping a present without seeing its price | Getting cash back for every present you don't like | Proving you paid for the whole birthday party | Getting a free gift the next time you visit the shop
exercise: There are 10 people in the queue and each takes 2 minutes. How long is the wait? | 20 minutes | 12 minutes | 10 minutes | 5 minutes
exercise: Why are queues longer when only one till is open? | Each till can serve only one person at a time | People walk slower when it's busy | The tills run out of money | Shoppers like waiting
exercise: Bella's mum forgets to scan £1.20 bread. If nobody notices, how much does the shop lose? | £1.20 | £0 | £2.40 | 20p
exercise: What should you do if you notice an item you forgot to scan? | Scan it and pay for it | Leave quickly before anyone sees | Put it back on any shelf | Hide it in your bag
exercise: Freddie's £12 toy arrives broken. The shop sends a new one free. How much extra does Freddie pay? | £0 | £12 | £24 | £6
exercise: Noah played a game for a week and just doesn't like it. Must the shop refund him? | No, it isn't faulty, he just changed his mind | Yes, shops must refund anything | Yes, but only half the price | No, games can never be returned even if broken
exercise: Milk is £1.20 each. They paid for 3 but got 2. How much were they overcharged? | £1.20 | £3.60 | £2.40 | 60p
exercise: A family saves 3 bags a week at 10p each. How much is that in 52 weeks? | £15.60 | £1.56 | £5.20 | £156
exercise: Why do shops charge for carrier bags? | So people use fewer and make less plastic rubbish | Because bags are made of gold | To pay the shop's rent | Because bags are a need
exercise: Leo's mate paid £2.50 for his ice cream at a cash-only van. What should Leo do? | Pay his mate back the £2.50 as promised | Pay back £1.25, because half seems fair enough | Forget it, because money between mates doesn't count | Pay back later with a promise of sweets instead
exercise: The kettle is £25 here and £20 down the road. If the shop price-matches, what does Mum pay? | £20 | £25 | £5 | £45
test: Josh's dad grumbles "bloody hell, only one till open." 12 people in front, 2 minutes each. How long will they wait? | 24 minutes | 14 minutes | 12 minutes | 20 minutes
test: Self-checkout yells "unexpected item in bagging area!" and Bella cracks up. What's the machine doing? | Checking the weight of what's put in the bag | Calling the police straight away for every shopper | Giving a free item to anyone who scans quickly enough | Taking a photo of you for a prize draw at the end
test: Noah to the shop: "I've played it a week and it's boring, gimme my £30." Shop: "It's not faulty, mate." Who's right? | The shop: changing your mind isn't a fault | Noah: boring counts as faulty | Noah: shops must refund anything | Both: he gets £15 back
test: Ava's mum ignores the receipt: "nah, never check." What would happen if she never checked? | Mistakes like the extra £1.20 milk would be missed | Shops would always give money back anyway | Receipts would stop printing | Prices would always be right
test: Lily's aunt, a proper Irish aunt, says "grand, sure I gave ye a gift receipt." ("Grand" means fine.) What can Lily do? | Swap the jumper for a bigger size | Sell the receipt for cash | Keep the jumper and get £15 too | Nothing, gift receipts are useless
test: Lady at the till: "wanna bag? that's 10p." Ruby: "nah, I'll carry it." Over a year, if the family skips 2 bags a week, how much do they save? | £10.40 | £5.20 | £20.80 | £1.04
test: Freddie's mum emails a photo of the broken toy. Why the photo? | It's proof the toy arrived broken | The shop collects photos of all the toys it sells | It makes the refund bigger | Photos are needed for any email
test: Ice-cream man: "card machine's broke, cash only, kid." Leo's mate pays. Next day Leo "forgets". What does that do? | Breaks his promise and his mate's trust | Nothing, owing is fine for ever | Makes the ice cream free | Makes his mate owe him
test: Ellie's mum asks for a price match on a £25 kettle. The other shop sells it for £5 less. What does she pay if they agree? | £20 | £25 | £30 | £15
test: Kian's mum lost the receipt for shoes that fell apart. What's likely? | The shop may refuse, or only offer a swap | The shop must give double the money back as a sorry | She gets the money back faster than with a receipt | The shop has to pay for a brand-new pair as well
test: Ava's receipt says 3 milks at £1.20 and they got 2. Her bro says "it's only a quid, who cares." What do they get back if they go in? | £1.20 | £1.00 | £3.60 | £2.40
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises a receipt? | A document evidencing the goods purchased, the date and the sum paid | A voucher entitling the bearer to a reduction upon a future purchase | A list of articles which a customer intends to acquire | A token determining a customer's place in a queue
exam: It is given that milk is priced at one pound twenty pence per carton and that three cartons are charged whereas two are received. Ascertain the sum overcharged. | One pound twenty pence | Three pounds sixty pence | Two pounds forty pence | Sixty pence
exam: Identify the circumstance in which a retailer is ordinarily obliged to provide a refund, repair or replacement. | Where the goods supplied are faulty | Where the purchaser has merely altered his opinion | Where the purchaser has mislaid the goods | Where the goods have been consumed in their entirety
exam: It is given that ten customers are queued and that each is served in two minutes. Determine the duration of the wait for a customer joining at the rear. | Twenty minutes | Twelve minutes | Ten minutes | Five minutes
